Q: Scraping Google search result links with Puppeteer A: <p>For starters, I don't see results for the selector <code>.rc .r</code>. Google selectors aren't always served consistently across all locales, and can change in the future the code below will eventually require adjustment.</p>
<p>As of 2025, Google has implemented strict bot detection that defeats previous versions of this answer, as well as other answers on this page. Using a Puppeteer-managed browser will likely be detected and blocked. Consider using their API or another search engine.</p>
<p>Here's a version that connects to a regular Chrome instance to bypass detection. You'll need to update the <code>chromePath</code> and other variables to work for your system (mine is MacOS 14.4).</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const {exec} = require(&quot;child_process&quot;);
const puppeteer = require(&quot;puppeteer&quot;); // ^24.20.0
const {setTimeout} = require(&quot;node:timers/promises&quot;);

const chromePath =
  &quot;/Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome&quot;;
const debuggingPort = 9223;
const userDataDir = &quot;/tmp/puppeteer-profile&quot;;

let browser;
(async () =&gt; {
  const cmd = `&quot;${chromePath}&quot; \
    --remote-debugging-port=${debuggingPort} \
    --user-data-dir=&quot;${userDataDir}&quot; \
    --no-first-run \
    --no-default-browser-check \
    about:blank`; // might not work: --headless=new \
  exec(cmd);

  for (let i = 0; i &lt; 100_000; i++) {
    try {
      browser = await puppeteer.connect({
        browserURL: `http://localhost:${debuggingPort}`,
      });
      break;
    } catch {
      await setTimeout(50);
    }
  }

  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.goto(&quot;https://google.com/&quot;, {
    waitUntil: &quot;domcontentloaded&quot;,
  });
  await page.locator('[title=&quot;Search&quot;]').fill(&quot;stack overflow&quot;);
  await page.keyboard.press(&quot;Enter&quot;);
  await page
    .waitForFunction(
      &quot;document.querySelectorAll('.LC20lb')?.length &gt; 5&quot;,
      {},
      {timeout: 3_000}
    )
    .catch(() =&gt; {});
  const links = await page.$$eval(&quot;.LC20lb&quot;, els =&gt;
    els.map(el =&gt; ({
      title: el.textContent,
      href: el.closest(&quot;a&quot;).href,
    }))
  );
  console.log(links);
})()
  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(err))
  .finally(() =&gt; browser?.close());
</code></pre>
<p>Another approach is to encode your search terms as a URL query parameter and navigate directly to <code>https://www.google.com/search?q=your+query+here</code>, saving a navigation and potential selector mishap. Here's an example of this using <a href="https://github.com/rebrowser/rebrowser-puppeteer" rel="nofollow noreferrer">rebrowser-puppeteer</a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import puppeteer from &quot;rebrowser-puppeteer&quot;; // ^24.8.1

const browser = await puppeteer.launch({
  headless: false,
  userDataDir: &quot;/tmp/puppeteer-profile&quot;,
  args: [&quot;--disable-blink-features=AutomationControlled&quot;],
});
const [page] = await browser.pages();
await page.goto(&quot;https://www.google.com/search?q=stack overflow&quot;, {
  waitUntil: &quot;networkidle0&quot;
});
const links = await page.$$eval(&quot;.LC20lb&quot;, els =&gt;
  els.map(el =&gt; ({
    title: el.textContent,
    href: el.closest(&quot;a&quot;).href,
  }))
);
console.log(links);
await browser.close();
</code></pre>
<p>See <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79667013/6243352">this answer</a> for additional antibot options.</p>
